===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|pressen||to crowd, thring, press}}, from {{der|en|fro|presser||to press}} (Modern {{cog|fr|presser}}) from {{der|en|la|pressāre}}, from ''[[pressus]]'', past participle of ''[[premere]]'' "to press". Displaced native {{cog|enm|thringen||to press, crowd, throng}} (from {{cog|ang|þringan||to press, crowd}}), {{cog|enm|thrasten||to press, force, urge}} (from {{cog|ang|þrǣstan||to press, force}}), {{cog|ang|þryscan||to press}}, {{cog|ang|þēowan||to press, impress}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+|+|+,prest}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[exert]] weight or force against, to act upon with force or weight; to exert [[pressure]] upon.
# {{lb|en|transitive|mechanics|electronics}} To activate a [[button]] or [[key]] by exerting a downward or forward force on it, and then releasing it.
#: {{syn|en|strike|hit|depress}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[compress]], [[squeeze]].
#: {{syn|en|thring|thrutch|Thesaurus:compress}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''press''' fruit for the purpose of extracting the juice}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[clasp]], [[hold]] in an embrace.
#: {{syn|en|hug}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Dryden Iliad|title=Hector and Andromache|page=456|passage=With tears and ſmiles ſhe took her ſon, and '''preſs'd''' / Th' illuſtrious infant to her fragrant breaſt.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[reduce]] to a particular shape or form by [[pressure]], especially [[flatten]] or [[smooth]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''press''' cloth with a [[clothes iron|clothes-iron]]}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''press''' a hat}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|sewing}} To flatten a selected area of fabric using an [[iron]] with an up-and-down, not sliding, motion, so as to avoid disturbing adjacent areas.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[drive]] or [[thrust]] by pressure, to [[force]] in a certain direction.
#: {{syn|en|thring|thrutch}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''press''' a crowd back}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|weightlifting}} To force [[weight]] away from the body by extending the arms or legs.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Men's Health|year=2026|issue=2|volume=41|title=Timeless Gains|author=Greg Presto|page=14
|text=Start with two weights on the floor. Grab one with your right hand, then curl or clean it to your shoulder, then '''press''' it overhead.}}
